{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Implementation note: INDEX_VERSION bumped 3 -> 4, not 2 -> 3\n\nRecording two post-review drifts in the plan's prose before this is pushed. Both are the\ntree moving under a reviewed plan, not findings against the plan's reasoning, and neither\nchanges an acceptance criterion.\n\n**1. The bump is 3 -> 4.** The plan's prose reads \"bumping `INDEX_VERSION` from 2 to 3 is\nthe whole migration\". `origin/main` already carries `INDEX_VERSION = 3`, so the commit\nbumps 3 -> 4. The AC is worded \"`INDEX_VERSION` ... is bumped\", with no target value, so it\nis satisfied as written -- the reasoning the prose gives for why the migration is free\n(`_ensure_schema` raises `StaleIndex` on mismatch, caller discards and rebuilds, no\n`ensure`-registry entry, no in-place `ALTER`) holds identically at 3 -> 4.\n\n**2. The AC's line citation is stale.** It cites `src/rebar/similar/index.py:39`.\nMeasured on `origin/main`: `INDEX_VERSION` is at **`:51`**; `:39` is `import hashlib`. In\nthe commit it sits at `:58` after the docstring addition.\n\n**The test was made version-agnostic rather than re-pinned.**\n`test_an_index_from_the_previous_version_is_discarded` previously hardcoded the strings\n`'2'` and `'3'`. Hardcoding `'3'`/`'4'` would have reintroduced exactly the breakage this\nbump caused -- the next schema change would have to edit the test again. It now imports\n`INDEX_VERSION` and writes `str(INDEX_VERSION - 1)`, asserting the reopen lands on\n`str(INDEX_VERSION)`. The test proves the discard-and-rebuild behaviour at whatever\nversion the tree is on, which is the behaviour the AC asks about.\n\n**Scope of the schema change, for the record:** `dirstate` gains a nullable `ticket_id`\ncolumn holding the key `_reindex` inserted the `docs` row under; NULL when the directory\nproduced no document. `_apply`'s removal reads it before deleting the `dirstate` row and\nthen deletes `docs` under `{dir_name} | {recorded ticket_id}` -- the same key set\n`_reindex` uses, so the two paths run identical key logic rather than agreeing by\ninspection. This is the plan's chosen fix (\"the key is recovered, not recomputed\"),\nunchanged.\n\nDiff: 4 files, +209/-26. `src/rebar/similar/catchup.py` +119/-25,\n`src/rebar/similar/index.py` +12/-1, plus tests.\nCommit `33bf192707f5af4ab316f76aedd3730c1a3c2d74`."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793647444557379013,"uuid":"94dae73d-dd81-4a26-af3f-a69a17844638"}